        </div>
        
        <!-- Content -->
        <article class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow p-8 mb-8 prose prose-lg">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Understanding Sensory Processing in Autism</h2>
            
            <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                Many autistic learners experience sensory processing differences that significantly impact their ability 
                to learn and engage in the classroom. What might seem like a minor detail—fluorescent lighting or background noise—
                can be overwhelming and distracting for students on the autism spectrum.
            </p>
            
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-6 mb-3">Common Sensory Sensitivities</h3>
            
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 space-y-2 mb-4">
                <li>Fluorescent or bright lighting</li>
                <li>Loud noises or unpredictable sounds</li>
                <li>Specific textures or materials</li>
                <li>Particular smells or scents</li>
                <li>Strong tastes or food textures</li>
                <li>Touch sensitivity or discomfort with physical proximity</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-6 mb-3">Practical Strategies for Sensory-Friendly Classrooms</h3>
            
            <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Lighting:</strong> Replace fluorescent bulbs with warm LED lighting or natural light. Allow students 
                to wear sunglasses if needed. Create a quiet corner with softer lighting for sensory breaks.
            </p>
            
            <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Sound Management:</strong> Use noise-canceling headphones or earplugs during noisy activities. 
                Create advance notice for loud activities (bells, assemblies). Provide a quiet space for students to regulate.
            </p>
            
            <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Workspace Organization:</strong> Minimize visual clutter. Use clear zones for different activities. 
                Provide flexible seating options (cushions, standing desks, wobble chairs).
            </p>
            
            <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Sensory Breaks:</strong> Build in scheduled breaks for movement, proprioceptive input, or quiet time. 
                Allow students to request breaks when needed. Provide fidget tools or movement opportunities.
            </p>
            
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-6 mb-3">The Impact of Sensory-Friendly Environments</h3>
            
            <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                When we create sensory-friendly classrooms, we don't just help autistic students—we benefit all learners. 
                A calm, organized environment supports executive function, reduces anxiety, and improves focus for everyone.
            </p>
            
            <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
                Most importantly, we send a powerful message: "Your needs matter. You belong here. We will make space for you."
            </p>
        </article>
        
